-- issue #1497: an interface METHOD whose name equals an UNCONSTRAINED PRELUDE STANDALONE,
-- applied to a receiver lying in BOTH denotations' domains, makes `medaka run` and the
-- BUILT BINARY print DIFFERENT values, both at exit 0, with no diagnostic anywhere.
-- `isEven` is stdlib/core.mdk:182 `export isEven : Int -> Bool` -- a standalone, NOT an
-- interface method, and carrying NO class constraint. The receiver 7 is inside that
-- standalone's domain (Int) AND at a head with a live `impl Parity Int`, so both
-- denotations are well-typed at the same receiver. That conjunction is the axis; arity is
-- not (arity-2 `xor` behaves identically) and neither is "receiver in both domains" alone
-- (the CONSTRAINED `sum` in the same shape is #1492's LOUD cell instead).
--
-- The `println "probe-reached-main"` line is the PIN's positive observable: it proves the
-- program was elaborated and started executing, so an unrelated failure earlier in the
-- pipeline (which prints nothing) cannot masquerade as this bug.
--
-- HAND-DERIVED expected answer: `True`. NOT captured from either engine -- the two engines
-- disagree here, so at most one could serve as an oracle and a capture would enshrine the
-- other one's wrong value. Derivation: under docs/spec/SHADOW-SEMANTICS.md S1-PRELUDE (a)
-- the implicit prelude is in NEITHER half of S1's left-operand kind partition, so a prelude
-- standalone creates no shadow in this module; `isEven` here is an ordinary interface-method
-- occurrence, `impl Parity Int` sits at the receiver's head, so it dispatches to the impl
-- and yields True. The built binary agrees; `medaka run` prints the prelude standalone's
-- False. This fixture pins run's WRONG value, which is what makes it self-draining.
interface Parity c where
  isEven : c -> Bool

impl Parity Int where
  isEven n = True

main =
  println "probe-reached-main"
  println (isEven 7)
